      "records": [
        {
          "id": "post-one",
          "title": "On designing with code",
          "slug": "designing-with-code",
          "format": "Note",
          "date": "2026-10-10",
          "status": "Published",
          "summary": "A small observation on computational workflows.",
          "body": "A model is more useful when its logic is as legible as its geometry. The interesting part is not just automating shapes—it is making design decisions inspectable.",
          "cover": "",
          "tags": [
            "Computation",
            "Process"
          ],
          "featured": true
        },
        {
          "id": "post-two",
          "title": "Introducing our visual research archive",
          "slug": "visual-research-archive",
          "format": "News",
          "date": "2026-10-06",
          "status": "Published",
          "summary": "A home for ongoing studies, experiments and fragments.",
          "body": "We are assembling a growing archive of geometric experiments, tools and material research. More soon.",
          "cover": "glyph",
          "tags": [
            "Studio",
            "News"
          ],
          "featured": true
        },
        {
          "id": "post-three",
          "title": "Geometry as a design language",
          "slug": "geometry-as-language",
          "format": "Article",
          "date": "2026-09-28",
          "status": "Published",
          "summary": "Why computational systems matter beyond form-making.",
          "body": "## Beyond the image\n\nComputational design is not simply a way to produce complex shapes. It is a way to describe relationships, dependencies and constraints.\n\n## A readable design process\n\nWhen the model can explain itself, we gain a new kind of design material: one that can be interrogated, adapted and shared.\n\n- Geometry\n- Metadata\n- Relationships\n- Operations\n\nEach becomes part of a continuous working system.",
          "cover": "facade",
          "tags": [
            "Research",
            "Computation"
          ],
          "featured": true
        },
        {
          "id": "post-four",
          "title": "A field of soft modules",
          "slug": "soft-modules",
          "format": "Note",
          "date": "2026-09-21",
          "status": "Draft",
          "summary": "An experiment with diffusion and repetition.",
          "body": "Can softness emerge from the proximity of hard-edged modular objects?",
          "cover": "field",
          "tags": [
            "Visual",
            "Brand"
          ],
          "featured": false
        }
      ]
